From 09ad0fb57f8dc8b5cf3b8a34256d08181ff44f2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:44:38 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: read old pages of the caller on CPUs without a
 hardware access flag

The recorder reads the caller's memory with the no-fault reads, which
fail on a page that is not present. A present page reads at once on
x86-64, whose hardware sets the accessed bit. An arm64 CPU without the
hardware access flag (ARMv8.0: the Cortex-A72 of a Raspberry Pi 4 and
of QEMU's DT boots) takes an access-flag fault on a present page whose
entry is old instead, and a no-fault read fails there. fork leaves every
page of the child old, so the execve of a freshly forked child had its
path, arguments and environment recorded as not resident (argv never
recorded, the program's command line lost) on such CPUs.

On arm64 each no-fault read of the recorder (user_read.h) is retried
once after the present, old pages of its range are marked accessed as
the access-flag fault marks them, under the conditions the not-resident
classification already has: task context, mmap_lock taken without
waiting. Pages that are not present still fail. Other architectures
call the kernel's reads as before: the header defines nothing there.
